# The advisories that were already open when the security gate was armed.
#
# Measured, not copied: `trivy fs --scanners vuln --severity HIGH,CRITICAL` run
# on a clean archive of origin/main 941d9cb4 on 2026-09-12 -- 2 targets,
# 143 finding rows, 107 distinct advisories, 139 HIGH and 4 CRITICAL rows.
# An earlier draft of this file was built from GitHub's Dependabot alert list
# instead, and did not describe what trivy reports: a baseline has to be
# generated by the tool that enforces it, or it exempts the wrong things and
# fails on the rest the first time it runs.
#
# THIS FILE MAY ONLY SHRINK. Adding a line here silences a NEW advisory, which
# is the one thing the gate exists to refuse. Removing a line is how the
# backlog is paid down. If a line must be added, it needs a founder decision
# and an ADR, not a commit.
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-17: "may only shrink" was a rule the guard did not
# enforce -- it checked a fixed ceiling of 107, so an id removed in one PR could
# be re-added in another. Now the entries must be a subset of
# scripts/trivy-baseline-2026-09-12.txt AND number exactly BASELINE_COUNT in
# scripts/check_security_gate_can_fail.py: removing a line means lowering that
# count in the same change, and re-adding one means raising it, visibly.]
#
# [CORRECTED 2026-09-17: a red is NOT "about something new" in general. Every
# line below is an advisory id with no path or package attached, and trivy
# forgives that id wherever it appears. Measured 2026-09-17 with trivy v0.74.0:
# a new services/new-service/requirements.txt pinning urllib3==2.1.0 and
# python-multipart==0.0.6 passes the gate (exit 0) while carrying HIGH rows
# for exactly those two packages. A red means an advisory id outside this list.
# Scoping the exemptions to (id, path) is a founder decision, open in ADR 0142.]
#
# The gate that reads this file is the 'Fail on a new high or critical
# advisory' step in .github/workflows/ci.yml. See ADR 0142.

